Rem ==================================================================
Rem shaker.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: reversible obfuscation
Rem
Rem Named for the machine that came first in a shoddy mill: the shaker
Rem tumbled the incoming rags to beat the dust and grit out of them
Rem before anything else was done. It rearranged what it was given and
Rem handed the same material on. That is this machine's whole ambition,
Rem and the name is deliberately not "crypto" — see WHAT THIS IS NOT.
Rem
Rem A four-round Feistel network over a list of Numbers, keyed by
Rem another list of Numbers, with a checksum appended so tampering is
Rem detectable. Numbers in, numbers out: no strings, no bytes, no
Rem bitwise operators — Shoddy has none of the last, and this machine
Rem wants none.
Rem
Rem     Let key    = { 91, 3355, 17, 40213 }
Rem     Let cipher = ShakeEncrypt({ 11, 22, 33, 44 }, key)
Rem     ShakeDecrypt(cipher, key)          ' { 11, 22, 33, 44 }
Rem     ShakeDecryptOr(cipher, wrongKey, { })
Rem
Rem Values are FIELD ELEMENTS: whole numbers in 0 .. ShakeModulus() - 1.
Rem Anything else is refused by name rather than quietly mangled. Text
Rem is not this machine's business, but it is one Map away — Asc each
Rem code unit in, Chr each one out.
Rem
Rem The guard-pair convention, as everywhere else in the library:
Rem ShakeDecrypt aborts when the checksum does not match, ShakeDecryptOr
Rem hands back your default, and ShakeVerify asks the question on its
Rem own.
Rem
Rem The tag catches TAMPERING, not the wrong key. It is computed over
Rem the ciphertext and knows nothing about your key, so decrypting with
Rem the wrong one passes the check and hands back plausible-looking
Rem nonsense. If you need to know the key was right, put something
Rem recognisable in the message and look for it afterwards.
Rem
Rem ---- WHAT THIS IS NOT --------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem This is an obfuscation layer, NOT a cryptosystem, and the difference
Rem is not a formality. The key schedule is a bare rotation through your
Rem key list, four rounds is far too few, the round function is cheap
Rem arithmetic rather than a studied S-box, and the checksum is a
Rem Fletcher-style sum that detects accidents rather than forgeries.
Rem Anyone who wants to read your data will read it. Use it to keep a
Rem save file from being edited in a text editor, not to keep a secret.
Rem
Rem ---- WHY THE MODULUS IS SMALL ------------------------------------
Rem
Rem Shoddy's only numeric type is the IEEE double, which holds integers
Rem exactly only up to 2^53. The round function squares its argument, so
Rem the modulus has to sit below 2^26 for that square to stay exact:
Rem 67108859 is the largest prime that does. Reaching for a 2^32-sized
Rem modulus, as is natural elsewhere, silently overflows into a region
Rem where the fractional part of a product is always zero — which is a
Rem mistake with no symptom at all, since the round function then
Rem returns 0 forever, every round degenerates to a swap, and an even
Rem number of rounds hands back the plaintext unchanged.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"

Rem ---- the field ---------------------------------------------------
Rem Machines cannot carry a top-level Let, so the constants are zero-arg
Rem Defs, as vt100's Esc() is.

Rem The largest prime below 2^26. Its square is 4.5e15, comfortably
Rem inside the double's exact-integer range of 2^53 = 9.0e15.
Def ShakeModulus() As Number
    67108859

Def ShakeRounds() As Number
    4

Rem x reduced into 0 .. modulus - 1. Floored, so negatives come back
Rem positive and subtraction is as safe as addition.
Def ShakeWrap(x As Number) As Number
    Let m = ShakeModulus()
    Let r = x - Floor(x / m) * m
    If r < 0 Then
        r + m
    Else
        r

Def ShakeIsField(x As Number) As Boolean
    x >= 0 And x < ShakeModulus() And x = Floor(x)

Def ShakeAllField(xs As List Of Number) As Boolean
    All(xs, ShakeIsField)

Rem ---- the round function ------------------------------------------
Rem Not a bijection, and it does not need to be: a Feistel network is
Rem invertible whatever its round function does, which is the whole
Rem charm of the construction. Every intermediate is kept under 2^52.

Rem The key list is walked round and round, so a key shorter than the
Rem message simply repeats — which is exactly as weak as it sounds, and
Rem is why the header says what it says.
Def ShakeKeyAt(keys As List Of Number, at As Number) As Number
    If IsEmpty(keys) Then
        Error("SHAKEKEYAT: THE KEY IS EMPTY")
    Else
        Nth(keys, 1 + ShakeWrapTo(at - 1, Length(keys)))

Rem n reduced into 0 .. width - 1, for indexing rather than arithmetic.
Def ShakeWrapTo(n As Number, width As Number) As Number
    Let r = n - Floor(n / width) * width
    If r < 0 Then
        r + width
    Else
        r

Rem x is under 2^26, so x * x is under 2^52 and exact; y is under 2^26,
Rem so y * 31 is under 2^31. Nothing here can reach 2^53.
Rem
Rem The offset matters: without it a small value and a small key give a
Rem small x, whose square is also small, so a message of zeros would come
Rem out barely disturbed. Shifting into the middle of the field first
Rem means even 0 with key 0 squares to something spread across it.
Def ShakeMix(v As Number, part As Number) As Number
    Let x = ShakeWrap(v + part + 33554393)
    Let y = ShakeWrap(x * x)
    ShakeWrap(y * 31 + x * 17 + 11)

Rem Encrypting round: (a, b) becomes (b, a + F(b)).
Def ShakeRoundFwd(a As Number, b As Number, part As Number) As Pair
    Pair(b, ShakeWrap(a + ShakeMix(b, part)))

Rem Its exact inverse: (a, b) becomes (b - F(a), a). Read it against the
Rem line above — the halves come back in the other order, and F is fed
Rem the half that survived the round rather than the one that changed.
Rem Getting this backwards still round-trips single blocks by accident
Rem when F is degenerate, which is how a broken cipher passes a test.
Def ShakeRoundBack(a As Number, b As Number, part As Number) As Pair
    Pair(ShakeWrap(b - ShakeMix(a, part)), a)

Def ShakeBlockEnc(a As Number, b As Number, keys As List Of Number, at As Number) As Pair
    Let p1 = ShakeRoundFwd(a, b, ShakeKeyAt(keys, at))
    Let p2 = ShakeRoundFwd(Fst(p1), Snd(p1), ShakeKeyAt(keys, at + 1))
    Let p3 = ShakeRoundFwd(Fst(p2), Snd(p2), ShakeKeyAt(keys, at + 2))
    ShakeRoundFwd(Fst(p3), Snd(p3), ShakeKeyAt(keys, at + 3))

Rem The same four keys, spent in the opposite order.
Def ShakeBlockDec(a As Number, b As Number, keys As List Of Number, at As Number) As Pair
    Let p1 = ShakeRoundBack(a, b, ShakeKeyAt(keys, at + 3))
    Let p2 = ShakeRoundBack(Fst(p1), Snd(p1), ShakeKeyAt(keys, at + 2))
    Let p3 = ShakeRoundBack(Fst(p2), Snd(p2), ShakeKeyAt(keys, at + 1))
    ShakeRoundBack(Fst(p3), Snd(p3), ShakeKeyAt(keys, at))

Rem A message of odd length has one value with no partner. Passing it
Rem through untouched would leave a plaintext number sitting in the
Rem ciphertext, so it is shifted by a pad derived from the key and the
Rem position — deterministic, and its own inverse under subtraction.
Def ShakePad(keys As List Of Number, at As Number) As Number
    ShakeMix(ShakeKeyAt(keys, at), at)

Rem ---- walking the message -----------------------------------------
Rem Accumulate with Prepend and Reverse once at the end. Append is
Rem Concat(xs, { x }) — O(n) per element, O(n^2) over a message.

Def ShakeEncList(xs As List Of Number, keys As List Of Number, at As Number, acc As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        Reverse(acc)
    Else
        If IsEmpty(Rest(xs)) Then
            Reverse(Prepend(ShakeWrap(First(xs) + ShakePad(keys, at)), acc))
        Else
            Let p = ShakeBlockEnc(First(xs), First(Rest(xs)), keys, at)
            ShakeEncList(Rest(Rest(xs)), keys, at + ShakeRounds(), Prepend(Snd(p), Prepend(Fst(p), acc)))

Def ShakeDecList(xs As List Of Number, keys As List Of Number, at As Number, acc As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        Reverse(acc)
    Else
        If IsEmpty(Rest(xs)) Then
            Reverse(Prepend(ShakeWrap(First(xs) - ShakePad(keys, at)), acc))
        Else
            Let p = ShakeBlockDec(First(xs), First(Rest(xs)), keys, at)
            ShakeDecList(Rest(Rest(xs)), keys, at + ShakeRounds(), Prepend(Snd(p), Prepend(Fst(p), acc)))

Rem ---- the tag -----------------------------------------------------
Rem Fletcher-style: a running sum and a running sum of sums, so a value
Rem moving position changes the tag where a plain total would not.
Rem Combined with a shift of 2^16, which keeps the product under 2^42.

Rem Everything but the tag on the end. Self-tail-recursive with an
Rem accumulator, so it compiles to a loop: seq's Taken builds its result
Rem with Prepend on the way OUT of the recursion, which costs a stack
Rem frame per element and overflows on a long message.
Def ShakeBody(xs As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    ShakeTakeN(xs, Length(xs) - 1, { })

Def ShakeTakeN(xs As List Of Number, n As Number, acc As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If n <= 0 Or IsEmpty(xs) Then
        Reverse(acc)
    Else
        ShakeTakeN(Rest(xs), n - 1, Prepend(First(xs), acc))

Def ShakeChecksum(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    ShakeSumLoop(xs, 0, 0)

Def ShakeSumLoop(xs As List Of Number, s1 As Number, s2 As Number) As Number
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        ShakeWrap(s2 * 65536 + s1)
    Else
        Let n1 = ShakeWrap(s1 + First(xs))
        ShakeSumLoop(Rest(xs), n1, ShakeWrap(s2 + n1))

Rem ---- the public words --------------------------------------------

Rem The ciphertext, one value per input value, with the checksum last —
Rem so the result is always one longer than the message.
Def ShakeEncrypt(xs As List Of Number, keys As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If IsEmpty(keys) Then
        Error("SHAKEENCRYPT: THE KEY IS EMPTY")
    Else
        If Not ShakeAllField(keys) Then
            Error("SHAKEENCRYPT: EVERY KEY VALUE MUST BE A WHOLE NUMBER IN 0 .. " & Str(ShakeModulus() - 1))
        Else
            If Not ShakeAllField(xs) Then
                Error("SHAKEENCRYPT: EVERY MESSAGE VALUE MUST BE A WHOLE NUMBER IN 0 .. " & Str(ShakeModulus() - 1))
            Else
                Let body = ShakeEncList(xs, keys, 1, { })
                Concat(body, { ShakeChecksum(body) })

Rem Is the checksum on the end still the one that belongs to the body?
Rem False for anything too short to carry a tag.
Def ShakeVerify(xs As List Of Number) As Boolean
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        False
    Else
        Last(xs) = ShakeChecksum(ShakeBody(xs))

Rem Strict: aborts if the message was altered, so a caller that ignores
Rem the possibility cannot proceed on rubbish.
Def ShakeDecrypt(xs As List Of Number, keys As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If IsEmpty(keys) Then
        Error("SHAKEDECRYPT: THE KEY IS EMPTY")
    Else
        If Not ShakeVerify(xs) Then
            Error("SHAKEDECRYPT: CHECKSUM MISMATCH — THE MESSAGE WAS ALTERED OR IS NOT A CIPHERTEXT")
        Else
            ShakeDecList(ShakeBody(xs), keys, 1, { })

Rem Total: the other half of the guard pair, for a ciphertext that
Rem arrived from somewhere you do not control.
Def ShakeDecryptOr(xs As List Of Number, keys As List Of Number, dflt As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If IsEmpty(keys) Or Not ShakeAllField(keys) Then
        dflt
    Else
        If Not ShakeVerify(xs) Then
            dflt
        Else
            ShakeDecList(ShakeBody(xs), keys, 1, { })
